{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause eviction (criminal-related causes)","requirement":"A landlord may evict for enumerated good causes, including a tenant's conviction or civil liability for assault, threats, theft, drugs or human trafficking; a three-day Notice to Quit must precede suit, and suit must be brought within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; this part states the causes in subsections (o)-(r), covering criminal convictions and civil liability for assault, threats, theft, drugs and human trafficking.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found by the court to be delinquent due to an offense involving assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, a member of the landl ord’s family or an employee of the landlord.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling eviction causes and notice","requirement":"To evict a tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling, the landlord must prove a listed cause and give three months' notice (at will or year-to-year tenancy), one month's notice (month-to-month) or a three-day notice for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating conduct.","key_value":"3 months' notice (at will/year-to-year); 1 month (month-to-month)","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal only by court order for the listed causes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law violation penalties","requirement":"A landlord who evicted for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months, and one who evicted to board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"3x damages; civil penalty up to $10,000; 5-year bar","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who vacate after a notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit or permanently remove the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord seeks to permanently board up or demolish the building or to permanently retire it from residential use, the landlord must not use this property for residential use for a period of five (5) years.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not take reprisal action, such as eviction, altering the lease or refusing to renew, against a tenant who exercises listed civil rights, including enforcing lease or health and safety rights, tenant organizing, or refusing a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all residential rentals except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or disabled veterans may receive protected tenancy against eviction for 40 years if their income is at most three times the county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40 years; income limit 3x county per-person average or $50,000","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans at least 60% disabled, who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings being converted who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","key_value":"Hudson County only","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"converted buildings in Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Notice to quit before eviction after foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a purchaser who wants to personally occupy the unit as a primary residence must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit before eviction, and a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties, including month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on excessive or unconscionable rent increases","requirement":"A rent increase in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, for property not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase, is prohibited landlord conduct.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental property subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where not subject to rent control, any residential rental.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Truth in Renting","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
